{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at `f3c79b186e8a1b516c8643660e272e91d1e6ba21`, tree `0eddadee`. The record shows that its 20 captured local refusals came from the 64-stream limit in dap's source, and it claims no more than that. The verdict stays bounded: it assigns neither the first run's 13 nor the older 30 missing bodies, finds no source fault about missing releases, and does not explain the hosted status 200 stream ending. One item below is for the planner's queue.\n\n**What I checked.** I read the note, `Streams.open`, the Worker's `/stream` route lines, the retained wrapper and the corrected run's events. The manifest hash and the three `SHA256SUMS` match the request. All 108 files match their hashes, all 14 table digests name manifest entries, the units' checksums pass, and no file is writable. My text scan for link, key, JWT and bearer patterns found nothing. I ran no scene and no test, since this head is documents only and changes no source.\n\n**Response provenance holds.** `diagnostic-worker.ts` clones a response only when the path is a `/stream` route and its status is already 503, and it returns the original. It imports the production `fetch.ts`, and it samples through the same `indexedScopes` binding. Its two `/_builder` paths answer only POST on 127.0.0.1, and the wrapper lives outside the tree. All 20 `.body` files hash to one value, and that file reads `{\"ok\":false,\"reason\":\"unavailable\"}`. `Streams.open` returns that reason only after the session checks pass and the count reaches `STREAMS = 64`. The wrapper's allowlist would also have kept `sessions-unavailable`, `clock-behind`, `history-unavailable` and `behind`, and none appears.\n\n**Native counts hold, and they add up.** The 20 refusal events' `nativeCounts` all show 64 open and 0 released. The event log shows 164 status 200 answers: 64 on the directory, 60 and 40 on the two issues. The last samples show exactly those open counts, and every release count stays 0, across 143 recorded browser request failures. So each stream the Worker opened still counts as open. All 20 refusals fall on the directory. Windows and waiters stay at 0, which is why this is dap's limit and no serving quota.\n\n**Limits hold.** The note reports the scenes' exit 1 under the unchanged console rule, and `captured-refusals-analysis.json` agrees: 35 checks pass, 1 fails, 100 rows open, no browser act. Worker and browser counts agree without per-request correlation, and the note says so. The note also says that sampling and cloning can affect timing. It makes no local-only claim.\n\n**Architecture: no source change.** The diff from main names the note and the manifest. The gate reuse at `658aab95` follows the document-only rule.\n\n**Security: no issue found.** The diagnostic endpoints exist only in the out-of-tree wrapper. The wrapper keeps a body only when it matches the safe shape and allowlist. Keys, profiles and raw output stay outside the units by the manifest's statement and my scan.\n\n**Simplification: nothing to remove.**\n\n**One item for the planner: the order of `01e2c4c4`.** Those counts say that no stream opened in this local run was released. If the same holds on a deployed Worker, each scope keeps its streams until their sessions stop qualifying, and a busy directory then refuses new streams. The Page falls back to polling. I did not trace what a refused `dap issue wait` does, and that wait is J5's wake path. Nothing here shows the deployed behavior. The request places `01e2c4c4` after the named later work, and that order was set before these counts existed in a reviewed record. `object.ts:835` and `object.ts:854` emit a metric with open and released counts on each open and release. If the hosted sink keeps it, a read of that metric answers the deployed half cheaply, with no new run. I recommend that read before the actor chip. The order is yours.\n\nJ5 acceptance stays closed, and Goose Desktop stays open. Neither depends on this verdict.","body":{"artifact":